    "id": 3933,
    "user": "Anita Wong",
    "role": "student",
    "text": "Better to omit the names of researchers and their studies but rather consider //what// they did. If people are interested they are just going to look up study and/or name of the researcher. Mentioning the name and/or study name can cause confusion and therefore make them drop the reading of your Expose and return to bibliography. You could rephrase the beginning of this paragraph like this: \"When ensuring that privacy concerns are met (what exact concerns by the way?), we are going to make use of already existing facts. There was... (and then here the description of what D'Amico did)\".",
    "annotationId": 3867,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "votes": [],
    "group": 1
  },
